#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""nura-to-vibeos: a Nura (postmarketOS) device build -> a VibeOS device zip, flashed from LineageOS recovery after
the VibeOS system zip (step 2 of VibeOS's two-step install).

  nura-to-vibeos [options] https://images.nura.eco/bpo/edge/oneplus-fajita/gnome-mobile/20261114-0517/
  nura-to-vibeos [options] ...-oneplus-fajita-boot.img.xz ...-oneplus-fajita.img.xz
  nura-to-vibeos [options] --device xiaomi-surya --panel huaxing ...-qcom-sm7150.img.xz

It keeps Nura's kernel and DTB as Nura boots them (the boot.img header version, addresses, page size and the
command line the kernel needs; pmOS's own parameters dropped, Plymouth off) and replaces postmarketOS's initramfs
with VibeOS's, which only finds the VibeOS root and starts it. The kernel's modules and all of the device's firmware
go into the zip's payload, with the device's settings a Debian/systemd VibeOS can use (UCM, udev rules, the sensors' mount
matrix, hexagonrpcd's firmware directory, the units pmOS disables) and Debian's builds of the Qualcomm daemons
(rmtfs, tqftpserv, hexagonrpcd, qrtr, qbootctl, 81voltd) for the first boot to install. Nothing from the images is
run: they are read as data (debugfs, mtools, python), never mounted.

A generic SoC image (qcom-sm7150: U-Boot and systemd-boot boot it, so it has no boot.img) needs --device: the boot
image is then built from the image's vmlinuz and the phone's DTB, in the header layout data/devices.txt gives, and
only that phone's firmware is carried. Where a phone came with different panels (POCO X3 NFC: Huaxing, Tianma),
--panel picks the DTB, and the zip refuses a phone whose bootloader names another panel.

In recovery the zip asks (volume keys, power) whether the boot image goes to this slot's boot (A/B phones; on
others, recovery) or nowhere, for a first start with "fastboot boot"; --boot-target fixes the answer. The partition
it overwrites is saved first, into the VibeOS root: /var/lib/vibeos/device/backup/.

Output, in --out (default: the current directory):
  vibeos-device-<nura-device>_<build>.zip     the device zip
  vibeos-device-<nura-device>_<build>-boot.img  its boot image, for "fastboot boot" (a first test writes nothing)
  vibeos-device-<nura-device>_<build>.txt     the report: inputs, boot image, what was carried over and what not
  nura-download/<device>-<flavour>-<build>/   a URL's downloads, kept (checked against the listing's sha256)

Needs (Debian 12 or later): python3, xz-utils, zstd, e2fsprogs (debugfs), mtools, dpkg (dpkg-deb), zip, gpgv and
debian-archive-keyring (the Debian packages are checked against the archive's signature); about 12 GB free in
--out while it runs. No root, no loop mounts, nothing written to a phone.
"""
